WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.340
And at night, I dreamed in green.

2
00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:06.774
Black screens, glowing
green lines, like Tron.

3
00:00:06.794 --> 00:00:11.668
AND gates, XOR gates, NAND gates,
waiting to be burned into a chip.

4
00:00:11.688 --> 00:00:13.588
Silicon Valley C Players.

5
00:00:13.608 --> 00:00:15.001
Fair warning.

6
00:00:15.021 --> 00:00:17.554
This is an embellished
fictional dramatization

7
00:00:17.574 --> 00:00:20.054
that may or may not be
based on true stories.

8
00:00:20.074 --> 00:00:22.553
The narrator is a guy
named Charlie Hack, and

9
00:00:22.573 --> 00:00:25.052
any resemblance to me
is mostly coincidental.

10
00:00:25.072 --> 00:00:29.236
Names, characters, companies and events
are fictitious, or used fictitiously.

11
00:00:29.256 --> 00:00:31.751
All three of us had the same dream.

12
00:00:31.771 --> 00:00:33.916
Real life bled into our dreams.

13
00:00:33.936 --> 00:00:37.238
Silicon Valley is very good at
telling a story about A players.

14
00:00:37.258 --> 00:00:40.561
The people who look like they
were always going to win, and the

15
00:00:40.581 --> 00:00:43.468
companies that look like they
were always going to work.

16
00:00:43.488 --> 00:00:47.453
The myth is a straight line: a
garage, a genius, a ticker symbol.

17
00:00:47.473 --> 00:00:51.196
But most of the real work looks
like another ordinary Tuesday.

18
00:00:51.216 --> 00:00:54.750
And from the outside, a C
thing can look like an A thing.

19
00:00:53.704 --> 00:00:56.223
A C player is not a failure.

20
00:00:56.243 --> 00:01:00.844
It's the person who was never the smartest
in the room, and kept going anyway.

21
00:01:00.864 --> 00:01:03.484
Because there is always the
smarter guy in the room.

22
00:01:03.504 --> 00:01:06.584
Good at everything
beats best at one thing.

23
00:01:06.604 --> 00:01:07.852
That's a C player.

24
00:01:07.872 --> 00:01:10.076
The Valley falls in love with products.

25
00:01:10.096 --> 00:01:12.967
I got more out of building
a platform: a foundation

26
00:01:12.987 --> 00:01:15.580
you can stand on, then
build the next thing on.

27
00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.329
You stack building blocks:
habits, fundamentals,

28
00:01:18.349 --> 00:01:21.583
the unglamorous layer
nobody puts on a slide.

29
00:01:21.603 --> 00:01:24.220
That layer is what you
actually stand on later.

30
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.020
The gymnastics foundation is the
hollow hold, just on the ground.

31
00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:30.669
Virtuosity in something
as simple as a hollow

32
00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:32.972
hold is what makes the next layer easy.

33
00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.908
Fundamentals, then building
blocks, then intensity.

34
00:01:35.928 --> 00:01:40.181
I had one opportunity that year, and
twenty weeks of training before it.

35
00:01:40.201 --> 00:01:43.050
There is a golden rule:
nothing new on race day.

36
00:01:43.070 --> 00:01:44.923
Why am I calm, cool, collected?

37
00:01:44.943 --> 00:01:46.972
Because it's just another Saturday.

38
00:01:46.992 --> 00:01:49.407
Then the work itself changed.

39
00:01:49.427 --> 00:01:52.829
I'm usually the most excited
person in the room about A.I.

40
00:01:52.849 --> 00:01:55.612
Which is funny, because
I should be the saddest.

41
00:01:55.632 --> 00:01:59.300
Because A.I. came after
the code writers first.

42
00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:03.479
In my own work, A.I. now writes
essentially all of the code I ship.

43
00:02:03.499 --> 00:02:06.368
That's how I work. Not
a measured team result.

44
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:08.212
Not a general industry figure.

45
00:02:08.232 --> 00:02:09.836
Most companies strive for well.

46
00:02:09.856 --> 00:02:11.358
I strive for fit.

47
00:02:11.378 --> 00:02:15.316
A.I. is even more powerful to those that
actually understand the fundamentals.

48
00:02:15.336 --> 00:02:18.750
Greg Glassman noticed that the
fittest person on earth might

49
00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:21.791
rank only in the 75th
percentile at any single event.

50
00:02:21.811 --> 00:02:24.944
Good at everything
beats best at one thing.

51
00:02:24.964 --> 00:02:28.096
C players are good and strong
fundamentally in multiple

52
00:02:28.116 --> 00:02:31.869
areas, and ultimately that's
what makes them a winner.

53
00:02:31.889 --> 00:02:33.952
That's the whole point of this book.

54
00:02:33.972 --> 00:02:37.611
The 75th-percentile C player who
stacks fundamentals across many

55
00:02:37.631 --> 00:02:40.708
areas beats the A player who
is best at just one thing.

56
00:02:40.728 --> 00:02:43.468
The book begins with
that green-screen dream.

57
00:02:43.488 --> 00:02:46.048
It ends by dreaming about A.I. It's free.

58
00:02:46.068 --> 00:02:49.348
It's open. The story
is still being written.

59
00:02:49.368 --> 00:02:51.349
More chapters are coming.

60
00:02:51.369 --> 00:02:53.981
And when version one is
ready, if you were around

61
00:02:54.001 --> 00:02:56.140
for any of it, you get to tell your side.

62
00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.884
Silicon Valley C Players. Coming in 2027.
